      "solution": "$u_8 = u_1 r^7 = -10$ ...(1) and $u_{12} = u_1 r^{11} = -160$ ...(2). Now $\\frac{u_1 r^{11}}{u_1 r^7} = \\frac{-160}{-10} \\implies r^4 = 16 \\implies r = \\pm \\sqrt[4]{16} \\implies r = \\pm 2$. If $r = 2$, then using (1), $u_1(2)^7 = -10 \\implies 128u_1 = -10 \\implies u_1 = -\\frac{10}{128} = -\\frac{5}{64}$. Thus $u_n = -\\frac{5}{64} \\times 2^{n-1}$. If $r = -2$, then using (1), $u_1(-2)^7 = -10 \\implies -128u_1 = -10 \\implies u_1 = \\frac{10}{128} = \\frac{5}{64}$. Thus $u_n = \\frac{5}{64} \\times (-2)^{n-1}$.",
